pad==0.2 |\n| GPL-3.0 | somelib==1.1 |\n\n**Flag: `somelib==1.1` (GPL-3.0) is a problem for a closed-source product.**\n\n- GPL-3.0 is strong copyleft. If you distribute a product that links to or incorporates `somelib`, the combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0, source included. That conflicts with shipping it closed.\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-tivoization and patent-licensing terms. These matter if you ship on devices or ship installers.\n- If the product is a hosted service that users never receive a copy of, plain GPL-3.0 isn't triggered. That would change if the license were AGPL. So the risk depends on how you ship.\n\n**Options before shipping**\n1. Replace `somelib` with a permissively licensed alternative.\n2. Ask the authors whether they offer a commercial or dual license.\n3. Remove it, or isolate it as a separate process that talks over a clean boundary. This is legally subtle, so get counsel to review it.\n4. Open-source your product under GPL-3.0, if that's acceptable.\n\n**The other two are fine, with small obligations**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0): include the license text and any NOTICE file in your distribution. The license also includes a patent grant.\n- `left-pad` (MIT): include the copyright and license notice.\n\n**Caveats**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies can carry their own licenses, including copyleft ones, so run a full scan, such as `pip-licenses` or the equivalent for your ecosystem, on the resolved tree.\n- I took the license labels from the file as given. I haven't checked them against each package's actual metadata.\n- This isn't legal advice. Have counsel review the GPL question before release.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}